WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:08.780
Larry Arnn: It is a student who introduces
our speaker today. This man is my teacher.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:13.280
I wanted to introduce him because of that,
and because any introduction or dealing with

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:22.400
him presents certain pitfalls that a grownup
can best handle, if anyone can handle them.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:28.400
Professor Jaffa is a famous conservative who
is often critical of other conservatives.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:36.809
I am perhaps the most experienced man on earth
in dealing with that problem, and I deal with

00:00:36.809 --> 00:00:44.039
it the best way possible, which is badly.
But on the other hand, it happens that in

00:00:44.039 --> 00:00:49.519
those respects I am on his side, and I'll
explain that just for a minute.

00:00:49.519 --> 00:00:55.149
To you students I'm going to tell you what
kind of teacher he is. My fondness for him

00:00:55.149 --> 00:01:04.180
starts with that. I met him in August of 1974,
29 years ago. I arrived in his home to pick

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:10.869
him up for the first class of my career in
graduate school. His arm was in a sling. He'd

00:01:10.869 --> 00:01:15.860
fallen off his bicycle. I learned that if
you wanted to get to know him, you could write

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:21.290
a really good paper, but it had to be brief,
which is a very difficult thing to do, and

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:28.290
then he would take notice of you. Or, you
could take up bicycle racing. That's hard

00:01:28.409 --> 00:01:32.290
to do, too, but even if you do it in a mediocre
way, if you have you heart in it, he'll like

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:39.040
you for it, so I did that.
He wasn't ready for class. He asked Peter

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:44.939
Schramm, now my brother-in-law, what time
the class was to be, and what the class was

00:01:44.939 --> 00:01:55.559
to be about. I won't say he's the best organized
teacher I ever had. We started reading Aristotle's

00:01:55.570 --> 00:02:02.359
Ethics. In the first sentence, there are four
words for human activity in Greek. I can tell

00:02:02.359 --> 00:02:06.649
you what they are now, and their definitions,
as well as I could back then, and it's 29

00:02:06.649 --> 00:02:13.649
years later. Praxis, prohairesis, dunamis
and techné. We spent about four weeks finding

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:20.390
out what those words meant, and the fact that
they describe every kind of human activity

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:24.370
that is voluntary.
Aristotle says in that first sentence that

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:31.319
every voluntary human action aims for the
good, and so the good is the thing we need

00:02:31.319 --> 00:02:38.709
to know how to define. In the second half
of the class, we read maybe eight more pages

00:02:38.709 --> 00:02:45.360
in the book. The book has ten books and we
didn't get all the way through book one, but

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:51.319
we thought we knew at the end what was the
problem of defining what is the good for the

00:02:51.319 --> 00:02:59.940
human being and how that relates to human
action. I liked it because it was a feeder

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:06.920
of high ambition and service, and because
it equipped one to talk about the most important

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:17.260
things in a certain way. I wasn't very good
at it, but I thought I was. For that, one

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:23.640
accumulates a debt to a teacher.
Professor Jaffa's an important man for other

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:34.720
reasons than that, but it's connected to this
idea of the good that he defends. He is probably

00:03:34.739 --> 00:03:38.970
most famous in public life for writing a certain
sentence in a speech that he wrote for Barry

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:46.610
Goldwater. "Extremism in defense of liberty
is no vice, moderation in pursuit of justice

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:54.829
is no virtue." Now, there are not ten college
campuses in the world where that sentence

00:03:54.829 --> 00:04:01.829
would be applauded. It was a disaster, many
people think, for Goldwater that he said that

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:08.450
sentence. But it's an interesting thing that,
at the 1984 Republican Convention, Ronald

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:16.209
Reagan, who was also a figure in the 1964
race, had that sentence repeated at the convention

00:04:16.209 --> 00:04:22.100
on the platform by Barry Goldwater. Perhaps
it was not so much a disaster as it was a

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:28.220
long-term victory.
Professor Jaffa knows his Aristotle and his

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:35.900
Thomas Aquinas and his Plato, and he knows
America. He sees in America an unbroken line

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:41.050
that goes from George Washington to Abraham
Lincoln. He thinks America is the greatest

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:49.510
country within the context of the discovery
of philosophy in Athens and the birth of universal

00:04:49.510 --> 00:04:57.700
monotheism in Jerusalem, and he is an enemy
of anyone who says otherwise. It does not

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:03.450
make any difference which supreme court upon
which they sit or which movement of which

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:10.770
they are a part, if they differ with him on
that, they differ with him on the most important

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:16.280
thing. He does not deny that some of the people
of whom he is critical have done wonderful

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:22.960
and even vital service for our country, but
his argument has always been that that thing

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:29.250
is central. It is the chord one plucks to
touch America. It is the thing that ennobles

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:35.910
it and makes it great.
I've asked myself, why have I been loyal to

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:45.950
him? I grew up in the South. My dad's a retired
school teacher. I grew up in and now I have

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:55.410
a Christian family. This man is a Northerner
and a Jew. When I was young, what I thought

00:05:55.420 --> 00:06:01.740
I liked about him the best was that there
were these high things one could come to understand,

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:09.800
and do something about them. But in my maturity,
I keep that thought, but I add to it another.

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:13.720
My father taught me that the greatness of
this country is that it treats ordinary folk

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:19.690
like him as well as kings, or maybe better
than kings, because it's a questionable thing

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:26.690
to be a king. My mother always said, "We taught
them right from wrong." She actually never

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:35.280
did, but she knew that those were solid things
and everyone knew what they were.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:42.120
When you get to college, those things become
very complicated. Professor Jaffa teaches

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:49.000
that they are complicated, but it is the function
of the people who have the capacity to investigate

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:56.000
those complications to defend those simple
things that are true that my mother and father

00:06:56.050 --> 00:07:03.050
thought were true. My teacher, Harry Jaffa.
Harry Jaffa: Thank you, Larry. This is an

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:24.910
unusual occasion for several reasons. The
title of the talk that I have in front of

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:33.950
me now is Our Embattled Constitution on Constitution
Day 2001. But this is 2003. What happened

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:41.720
in between? Most of you here are probably
much too young to remember a time when the

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:47.930
Catholic Legion of Decency ruled Boston, but
there was a time when it was every publisher's

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:57.360
ambition to have his book banned in Boston.
That was not an option for me, but I did the

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:05.240
next best thing, or maybe something better,
I was banned at Hillsdale. However, it may

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:15.880
be that the time is riper now than it was
then. I'll read this speech because I wrote

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:24.990
it two years ago, a little more than two years
ago, and I know that what I've written down

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:33.470
is what I should say or what I thought I should
say, but I'll interrupt my reading with some

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:39.440
comments which I think may make the text more
alive than it otherwise would be.

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:45.780
The unique power of Lincoln, of the Lincoln
theme, is suggested by the fact that it has

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:52.620
occasioned more titles in the world's libraries
than any other name. For some time, it has

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:59.010
been one of the three most numerous, the other
two being Jesus Christ and William Shakespeare.

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:04.780
Recently it has surpassed the others. This
is even more remarkable when one considers

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:10.590
the comparative shortness of time since Lincoln’s
life as compared to Jesus Christ and William

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:19.680
Shakespeare. It's also remarkable that Allen
Guelzo, in his 1999 book, Abraham Lincoln:

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:23.950
Redeemer President, declared that Crisis of
the House Divided, that was my book on the

00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:29.750
Lincoln-Douglas debates published in 1959,
was “incontestably the greatest Lincoln

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:35.770
book of the century.” I hasten to point
out that this is not a consensus view, but,

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:42.330
as Thomas Aquinas would say, what is evident
to the wise is not evident to all.

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:47.890
Whatever the rank of Crisis, it is now supplemented
by its sequel, A New Birth of Freedom: Abraham

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:53.330
Lincoln and the Coming of the Civil War. What
is unique about both books in the Lincoln

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:59.240
literature, is that I have taken Lincoln’s
teaching about the Declaration of Independence

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:05.550
as Lincoln himself regarded it, as a standard
not merely for Lincoln’s time, but for all

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:12.280
time. I have done this, not merely as agreeing
with Lincoln, but as a matter of demonstrable

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:16.950
philosophic truth.
When I began my work on the Lincoln-Douglas

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:23.150
debates in 1946, there had never been any
attempt to describe or analyze the arguments

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:29.250
put forth in those debates. To the historians,
they were merely links in the chain of causes

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:37.340
that brought Lincoln to power. The scholarly
consensus then was that the Civil War came

00:10:37.340 --> 00:10:43.280
about because of unscrupulous politicians
on both sides of the slavery issue seeking

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:49.930
political advantage who had inflamed public
opinion until compromise became impossible.

00:10:49.930 --> 00:10:56.930
Of all those who rode to power by exploiting
the slavery question, the most prominent was

00:10:56.970 --> 00:11:05.230
Lincoln. He was regarded simply as the most
successful of the unscrupulous.

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:12.230
My book, I must say, is, in the sense in which
I'm explaining it now, the first book on Lincoln

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:20.400
which was favorable to Lincoln. Despite Lincoln's
great reputation and veneration and the Lincoln

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:27.120
in the Greek temple in Washington, the literature
on Lincoln has not been favorable to Lincoln.

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:35.100
Douglas, his opponent in the 1858 senatorial
contest, tried vainly, it was said by the

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:40.040
historians, to dampen the passions over slavery
by his doctrine of popular sovereignty. By

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:44.980
letting the people of each territory decide
for themselves whether or not to have slavery

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:51.970
among their domestic institutions, the slavery
question would be confined to the territories

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:56.820
and kept out of Congress.
In my book on the Lincoln-Douglas debates,

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:03.180
I presented the case with Douglas himself
I think more powerfully than any of the people

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:11.990
who were pro-Douglas, because it was a real
alternative. The slavery question would thus

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:18.990
cease to agitate the nation as a whole. Douglas,
by ignoring or denying the immorality of slavery,

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:24.240
was seen as the more moral of the two. He
was thus a model statesman, someone who could

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:29.750
calm the turbulent waters. This was the view
of Lincoln dominant before the publication

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:36.300
of Crisis of the House Divided.
In 1946, the prevalent academic view of moral

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:42.550
questions was that they were insoluble by
reason. By considering slavery a question

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:47.750
to be decided by self-interest rather than
morality, Douglas was thought to be actually

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:55.279
on the side of morality. By so doing, he made
the uncompromisable compromisable. Lincoln,

00:12:55.279 --> 00:13:00.540
by insisting that the moral condemnation of
slavery had to be the basis of all public

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:08.460
policy concerning it, was held to be a herald
of unreason, of passion and of war.

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:13.440
I believe I was the first, and in a sense
remain the only one, to defend Lincoln on

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:18.750
Lincoln’s own ground. I have done so by
taking the self-evident truths of the Declaration

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:26.320
of Independence, as did Lincoln, as assertions
of right reason, and not of opinion merely.

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:31.040
And I have maintained with Lincoln that right
reason, no less than Scripture, is the voice

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:36.880
of God. According to Lincoln, those who would
deny freedom to others could not, under a

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:42.820
just God, long remain in possession of their
own. When Lincoln said that, as he would not

00:13:42.820 --> 00:13:48.790
be a slave, so he would not be a master, he
was saying neither more nor less than Jesus

00:13:48.790 --> 00:13:55.339
when he said, “Whatsoever you would that
others do unto you, do you unto them.” Prophecy

00:13:55.339 --> 00:14:00.730
was with us then. It is with us yet, if we
would hear it.

00:14:00.730 --> 00:14:06.820
Academic opinion on the rationality of morality
has become much worse in the 40 years since

00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:13.380
Crisis. Campuses across the country are in
the grip of something called political correctness.

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:20.920
A main feature of PC is something called cultural
relativism or diversity. Since we are not

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:27.430
supposed to really know right from wrong,
we show our sophistication by patronizing

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:33.910
indifferently the different concepts of right
and wrong as they manifest themselves in different

00:14:33.910 --> 00:14:42.630
cultures or different ways of life. Unfortunately,
some cultures celebrate, among other horrors

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:49.630
too numerous to mention, human sacrifice,
suttee, cannibalism and slavery.

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:55.380
So political correctness arbitrarily rules
out those cultures it does not like, and morality

00:14:55.380 --> 00:15:01.210
becomes a matter of what you like. Since reason
is held to be impotent, it is replaced by

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:08.380
passionate commitment, as it was in the Third
Reich. Theoretical indifference thus mutates

00:15:08.380 --> 00:15:17.690
into blind partisanship. We see the effects
of this all around us. Perhaps it is most

00:15:17.690 --> 00:15:25.900
conspicuous in heterosexual and homosexual
promiscuity and, in general, in the disintegration

00:15:25.900 --> 00:15:32.350
of the monogamous family. We see it as well
in the rise of an environmental movement,

00:15:32.350 --> 00:15:38.460
which, like communism, claims the authority
of a spurious science as a means to despotic

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:45.779
control of our lives.
But the bad news is not only from the campuses.

00:15:45.779 --> 00:15:52.130
My 1994 book, Original Intent and the Framers
of the Constitution, and my 1999 book, Storm

00:15:52.130 --> 00:15:58.440
Over the Constitution, record the complete
alienation of conservative jurisprudence from

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:03.880
the principles of the founding and of Abraham
Lincoln. The classic text in this regard is

00:16:03.880 --> 00:16:11.920
the following. "If a democratic society adopts
a constitution," this is now a quotation from

00:16:11.930 --> 00:16:17.690
a great authority who I will identify in a
moment, "If a democratic society adopts a

00:16:17.690 --> 00:16:25.330
constitution and incorporates in that constitution
safeguards for individual liberty, these safeguards

00:16:25.330 --> 00:16:32.300
do indeed take on a generalized moral rightness
or goodness. They assume a general social

00:16:32.300 --> 00:16:40.460
acceptance neither because of any intrinsic
worth nor because of any unique origins in

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:46.440
someone’s idea of natural justice, but instead
simply because they have been incorporated

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:51.690
in a constitution."
The foregoing is from Chief Justice Rehnquist’s

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:57.450
celebrated essay on “The Notion of a Living
Constitution.” The contemptuous reference

00:16:57.450 --> 00:17:04.130
to “someone’s idea of natural justice”
is all the consideration he gives to the Constitution

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:12.370
of Madison, Jefferson, Marshall and Lincoln.
This reflects as well the dominant irrationality

00:17:12.370 --> 00:17:18.959
of the academic climate within which Lincoln
historians, and American historians generally,

00:17:18.959 --> 00:17:24.730
have pursued their vocation. Consider the
implications of what the Chief Justice has

00:17:24.730 --> 00:17:31.290
asserted. If safeguards for individual liberty
do not have “any intrinsic worth,” then

00:17:31.290 --> 00:17:41.309
neither does individual liberty, nor individual
life. This is pure nihilism. The illusion

00:17:41.309 --> 00:17:48.300
of morality, and it is here regarded only
as an illusion, has as its cause nothing but

00:17:48.300 --> 00:17:56.030
the will of the people who have adopted it.
Now, the Constitution of 1787, besides safeguards

00:17:56.030 --> 00:18:02.940
of individual liberty, had safeguards of slavery.
These were adopted by the same people at the

00:18:02.940 --> 00:18:09.800
same time, and hence on Mr. Justice Rehnquist’s
premises, these safeguards of slavery took

00:18:09.800 --> 00:18:18.870
on the same generalized moral rightness or
goodness as the safeguards of liberty. This

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:27.430
is exactly the position of the seceding states
in 1860 and 1861. By slavery to be a moral

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:35.670
wrong, the Republican Party, they contended,
had violated the consensus by which the Constitution

00:18:35.670 --> 00:18:41.600
had been ratified and the Union formed. This
was the position against which Lincoln had

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:47.640
to contend. It's interesting that the present
Chief Justice has stated exactly the position

00:18:47.640 --> 00:18:54.429
of the seceding South.
How can a people, any people, adopt a constitution?

00:18:54.429 --> 00:19:00.850
It must be by some electoral process. In that
electoral process, must there not be freedom

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:07.140
of speech and of the press, and of the people
peaceably to assemble? Must these rights not

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:14.120
be recognized beforehand for the election
to have any validity? Must it not be recognized,

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:20.000
a priori, that the majority has no right to
decide how the citizens may worship their

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:27.920
God? Must it not be understood, a priori,
that the majority may not enslave or expropriate

00:19:27.920 --> 00:19:34.210
the minority or drive them into exile?
The entire concept of legitimate majority

00:19:34.210 --> 00:19:44.150
rule is bounded on all sides by a priori reasons,
within which alone majority rule may be legitimized.

00:19:44.150 --> 00:19:50.370
The sum of all these a prioris is embodied
in what the Founders understood to be the

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:57.690
social contract, by which majority rule is
authorized. Consent is given thereby, not

00:19:57.720 --> 00:20:04.720
to the powers of government, but to the just
powers of government. This also is Lincoln’s

00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:11.870
teaching. On the other hand, Rehnquist’s
idea of unbounded majority rule, of unbounded

00:20:11.870 --> 00:20:17.010
popular will, is perfectly consistent with
the plebiscite, which has been the instrument

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:23.020
of legitimacy of tyrants from Napoleon to
Hitler and Stalin.

00:20:23.020 --> 00:20:30.549
Mr. Justice Scalia, following Mr. Justice
Rehnquist, also declares that, “The whole

00:20:30.549 --> 00:20:37.749
theory of democracy is that the majority rules.
That is the whole theory of it. You protect

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:42.549
minorities only because the majority determines
that there are certain minority positions

00:20:42.549 --> 00:20:48.200
that deserve protection.” But what if the
majority does not elect to protect minority

00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:56.760
positions, or that some minorities are protected
but not others? In 1857, the Supreme Court,

00:20:56.780 --> 00:21:02.890
speaking through Chief Justice Taney, declared
that majority opinion at the time of the ratification

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:09.890
of the Constitution held that black men and
women were “so far inferior that they had

00:21:09.890 --> 00:21:15.670
no rights which white men were bound to respect,”
and that they might be reduced to slavery

00:21:15.670 --> 00:21:22.059
for their own benefit. The proposition that
all men are created equal was not, he falsely

00:21:22.059 --> 00:21:26.299
asserted, understood to include black human
beings.

00:21:26.299 --> 00:21:32.710
Now, Taney was wrong about opinion at the
time of the founding. But it was certainly

00:21:32.710 --> 00:21:38.030
true, on the eve of the Civil War, that white
majorities in most, if not all, of the slave

00:21:38.030 --> 00:21:43.660
states believed in the inferiority of Negroes,
and did not believe that Negroes, whether

00:21:43.660 --> 00:21:51.220
free or slave, were entitled to constitutional
protection for their lives, liberties or property.

00:21:51.220 --> 00:21:57.390
And it is precisely this point of view, whether
he knows it or not, that Mr. Scalia endorses

00:21:57.390 --> 00:22:04.630
when he says that, in a democracy, minorities
depend upon the majority for their rights.

00:22:04.630 --> 00:22:10.250
Consider also how this view of minority rights
would today justify “ethnic cleansing,”

00:22:10.250 --> 00:22:15.630
by which whole peoples are obsessed in the
Balkans, in Africa, and in Asia. And let us

00:22:15.630 --> 00:22:21.500
not forget the role of know-nothingism in
our own history.

00:22:21.500 --> 00:22:27.880
The struggle over the Constitution today is
between those who believe in a “living constitution”

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:33.520
and those who profess their allegiance to
a jurisprudence of original intent. If the

00:22:33.520 --> 00:22:39.080
former, that's those who believe in a living
constitution, the former think that the original

00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:46.040
Constitution is a mere legacy of a reactionary
past, featuring slavery, the subjection of

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:53.250
women, capital punishment, and economic and
social inequality, their constitution is one

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:58.830
in which a wise Supreme Court can order wise
constitutional remedies for the endless list

00:22:58.830 --> 00:23:04.850
of alleged wrongs. Since there is no limit
to what may be alleged to be wrong, there

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:11.490
is no limit to what may be a constitutional
remedy. This is unlimited government by a

00:23:11.490 --> 00:23:18.470
judicial oligarchy, a virtual negation of
everything the Founders believed. In substance,

00:23:18.470 --> 00:23:24.350
the jurists of this living constitution have
seceded from the Union of the Founders, as

00:23:24.350 --> 00:23:31.870
completely as did the slave states that formed
the Confederacy in 1861.

00:23:31.870 --> 00:23:40.570
We can save the Constitution only by restoring
to it a genuine jurisprudence of original

00:23:40.570 --> 00:23:46.510
intent. This can only be done, as Lincoln
did it, by distinguishing the principles of

00:23:46.510 --> 00:23:53.510
the Constitution of 1787 from the compromises
of the Constitution. Unlike William Rehnquist

00:23:54.980 --> 00:24:00.549
or John C. Calhoun, Abraham Lincoln believed
that safeguards of individual liberty were

00:24:00.549 --> 00:24:05.929
indeed possessed of intrinsic worth. They
were possessed of intrinsic worth because

00:24:05.929 --> 00:24:12.929
each individual human being was endowed by
his creator with unalienable rights. Without

00:24:13.390 --> 00:24:19.030
question, these principles condemned slavery.
But slavery was deeply intertwined with the

00:24:19.030 --> 00:24:26.030
roots of colonial society and could not quickly
or easily be extirpated. Slavery was perhaps

00:24:26.110 --> 00:24:32.480
the oldest institution of human society next
to the family. No attempt was ever made in

00:24:32.480 --> 00:24:38.950
the ancient world to abolish it, notwithstanding
the importance of individual liberty to the

00:24:38.950 --> 00:24:45.950
greatest of the Greeks and Romans. The compromises
with slavery in the Constitution of 1787 were

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:52.669
means necessary for the ratification of the
Constitution, and the ratification of the

00:24:52.669 --> 00:24:59.669
Constitution, even with its compromises, was
morally justified, because every alternative

00:25:02.410 --> 00:25:07.710
to the Constitution would have been far more
favorable to slavery.

00:25:07.710 --> 00:25:12.980
We must remember that the Founding Fathers,
like ourselves and every generation of mankind,

00:25:12.980 --> 00:25:19.980
was born into a world they did not make. Yet
no generation did more to remake for the better

00:25:21.730 --> 00:25:28.730
the world 
they inherited. However impatiently and unhistorically
we look upon our past, the fourscore and seven

00:25:37.690 --> 00:25:43.559
years that separate the Declaration of Independence
from the Gettysburg Address, seen in the light

00:25:43.559 --> 00:25:50.200
of all human history, is a remarkably short
time in which to have accomplished the great

00:25:50.200 --> 00:25:56.370
work of emancipation.
Today, nearly every good thing that we enjoy,

00:25:56.370 --> 00:26:01.970
and nearly every good thing that the world
enjoys because of us, we owe to the principles

00:26:01.970 --> 00:26:08.559
enshrined in our founding, the principles
preserved by Abraham Lincoln. It is shameful

00:26:08.559 --> 00:26:13.730
that at the very moment when these principles
are having their greatest success, we witness

00:26:13.730 --> 00:26:20.290
their abandonment. Nowhere is the genesis
of this intellectual and moral breakdown more

00:26:20.290 --> 00:26:27.290
visible than in the scholarly writing on Abraham
Lincoln. The logic of Lincoln’s commitment

00:26:28.429 --> 00:26:35.299
to the principles of the Declaration of Independence
has, however, never been refuted. It has rather

00:26:35.299 --> 00:26:41.590
been ignored, and that ignorance treated as
if it were a refutation.

00:26:41.590 --> 00:26:46.600
Our opponents are, however, tied by interests
that they cannot admit to arguments that they

00:26:46.600 --> 00:26:53.600
cannot defend, and the alienation of conservatives
is not less than that of liberals. The struggle

00:26:54.960 --> 00:27:01.940
for truth will be hard, and the fight will
not only be with the weapons of truth. The

00:27:01.940 --> 00:27:07.200
end of this deadly ignorance and alienation
is not at hand. This is not the beginning

00:27:07.200 --> 00:27:12.520
of the end. It is not even the end of the
beginning. But the battle has been joined.

